# Fibers & Textile Technologies Curriculum Cache
#
# Cellulose hydrogen bonding, wool disulfide crimp, Jacquard punch-card computing, and the global cotton empire.

#>spw_cache_mat_textiles
#:cache #!fibers_and_textile_technologies
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model the macromolecular chemistry of natural and synthetic textile fibers, yarn friction mechanics, the Jacquard loom computing precursor, and the world history of the global cotton empire."
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"macromolecular_fiber_chemistry_and_mechanics"{
  "cellulosic_and_proteinaceous_natural_polymers": .{
    cellulose_microfibrillar_architecture: "Cotton and linen (flax): linear beta-1,4-D-glucan polysaccharide chains (degree of polymerization DP ~10,000 in native cotton); intense intra-chain (O3-H...O5) and inter-chain (O6-H...O3) hydrogen bonding networks yielding crystalline Cellulose I_beta fibrils; increases in tensile strength when wet due to water-lubricated internal stress redistribution",
    wool_keratin_disulfide_springs: "Alpha-helical protein coiled-coils linked by covalent cystine disulfide bridges (-S-S-); cuticular overlapping scales producing directional friction effects (felting); three-dimensional crimp conferring superior natural elastic recovery and thermal loft",
    silk_fibroin_antiparallel_beta_sheets: "Bombyx mori sericulture: non-covalent antiparallel beta-pleated sheets rich in alternating glycine-alanine repeats; dense van der Waals packing generating exceptional tensile strength (~0.6-1.2 GPa) rivaling structural steel wire with fluid drape",
  }[reg=facet],
  "synthetic_polyamides_and_aramids": .{
    nylon_66_condensation_polymers: "Synthesized by Wallace Carothers at DuPont (1935): polycondensation of adipic acid and hexamethylenediamine; linear aliphatic carbon chains aligned by regular inter-chain amide hydrogen bonding; high abrasion resistance and elastic recovery",
    polyethylene_terephthalate_pet_and_kevlar: "PET polyester: melt-spun hydrophobic filament resisting wrinkling; Kevlar (poly-p-phenylene terephthalamide): liquid crystalline aramid solution spinning; rigid aromatic phenylene rings producing ultra-stiff planar arrays for ballistic body armor",
  }[reg=facet],
  "textile_yarn_and_weaving_mechanics": .{
    spinning_friction_and_twist_insertion: "Amontons-Coulomb friction laws: converting short staple cotton fibers (25-35mm) into continuous tensile yarn via mechanical drafting and helical twist insertion (Z-twist vs S-twist); fiber-to-fiber radial compressive friction preventing slippage under axial load",
    harness_loom_interlacing: "Orthogonal interlacing of lengthwise Warp yarns under mechanical tension and horizontal Weft picks; fundamental weave architectures (Plain, Twill, Satin) governing porosity, tear strength, and drape",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"world_history_and_the_textile_engines_of_civilization"{
  "the_silk_road_and_the_medieval_wool_guilds": .{
    chinese_sericulture_state_monopoly: "Han Dynasty capital punishment for exporting silkworm eggs or mulberry seeds; overland Silk Road and maritime spice routes connecting Chang'an with Rome and Persia; silk serving as international currency, diplomatic tribute, and bureaucratic salary",
    the_florentine_wool_trade_and_the_renaissance: "English raw wool exported to Flanders and Florence; the Arte della Lana guild finishing luxury woolens; textile merchant capital directly financing the rise of the Medici bank and the Italian Renaissance",
  }[reg=facet],
  "the_transatlantic_cotton_empire_and_industrialization": .{
    the_dark_triad_of_cotton: "The first Industrial Revolution sparked not by coal or steam, but by cotton: Hargreaves' spinning jenny (1764), Arkwright's water frame (1769), and Crompton's mule multiplying yarn output ten-thousand-fold in Manchester ('Cottonopolis')",
    eli_whitneys_cotton_gin_and_enslavement: "The 1793 cotton gin mechanizing green-seed upland cotton separation; entrenching and violently expanding chattel slavery across the American plantation South to feed the insatiable demand of British mechanized mills",
  }[reg=facet],
  "jacquard_punched_cards_and_the_birth_of_computing": .{
    joseph_marie_jacquard_1804: "Automating complex patterned silk weaving by controlling loom needles via binary punched paper card chains; holes permitting warp thread lifts, blanks blocking them",
    the_lovelace_babbage_computational_epiphany: "Charles Babbage incorporating Jacquard punch cards into the Analytical Engine; Ada Lovelace's prophetic 1843 realization: 'The Analytical Engine weaves algebraical patterns just as the Jacquard-loom weaves flowers and leaves'—textile mechanics birthing modern software",
    the_nylon_craze_and_petrochemical_clothing: "DuPont's 1940 release of nylon stockings selling 64 million pairs in months; requisitioned overnight for WWII parachutes, tire cords, and ropes; permanently shifting global human clothing from solar agricultural cycles to fossil fuel petrochemical feedstocks",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"wonder_probes"{
  #>wonder_mat_textiles_1
  ?["Why is cotton fabric significantly stronger when thoroughly soaked in water, whereas paper (made of the same cellulose polymer) disintegrates into mush?"]{
    #:depth #!molecular ~#lens(crystalline_microfibrillar_plasticization)
    ~#hypothesis: "Raw cotton fibers are twisted, hollow ribbon-like tubes composed of long, highly crystalline cellulose microfibrils (>70% crystallinity). In the dry state, internal stresses concentrate at twisted kinks, causing brittle premature rupture. Water molecules penetrate only the amorphous regions, acting as a molecular plasticizer that allows individual cellulose microfibrils to untwist, slide, and align uniformly along the tensile axis, distributing the load across millions of crystalline hydrogen bonds before breaking. Paper lacks this long-fiber orientation and relies on fragile inter-fiber surface hydrogen bonds that water instantly dissolves."
    !probe{ "Measure tensile breaking load and microfibrillar alignment in single cotton fibers vs wood-pulp paper strips across 0% to 100% relative humidity." }
    $%[textiles.cotton_wet_tensile_strength_increase_percentage, hydrogen_bonding.paper_wet_strength_retention_ratio]
  }
}
